_apply_udev_rules() {
    # pacman's systemd hook reloads the rule files, but already-connected
    # devices keep the permissions they were given at plug time. A trigger
    # re-runs the rules against them - which matters here because a Corsair
    # cooler sits on an internal USB header and cannot simply be replugged.
    udevadm control --reload-rules >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=usb --subsystem-match=hidraw >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

post_install() {
    _apply_udev_rules
    cat <<'EOF'

Corsair Control installed. The udev rules were applied to devices that are
already connected, so no replugging or reboot should be needed.

  1. Try it:        corsair-control --demo
  2. Your hardware: corsair-control --list
  3. Start it:      corsair-control

If step 2 reports "permission denied", reboot once and try again.

Mainboard fan headers (hwmon) can only be written as root. If you want those
controlled too, enable the background service:

  sudo systemctl enable --now corsair-controld

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
    _apply_udev_rules
    cat <<'EOF'

Corsair Control upgraded. If the service is running, restart it:

  sudo systemctl restart corsair-controld

EOF
}

post_remove() {
    cat <<'EOF'

Your profiles were kept in ~/.config/corsair-control (and /etc/corsair-control
when the service was used). Delete them by hand if you no longer need them.

EOF
}
